  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Find the coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ in the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2+x)$ about $x=0.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ is\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "The coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ is $\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{(3)}(0).$ Let's compute the necessary derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2+x) \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2+x} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{(2+x)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{\u2034}(x) & =\\frac{2}{(2+x)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate ${f}^{\u2034}(0),$ which gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2034}(0)=\\frac{2}{(2+0)^{3}}=\\frac{2}{8}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{\u2034}(0)=\\frac{1}{3!}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{24}.\n$$\n\nSo, the coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ is $\\frac{1}{24}.$",
